2:T149a,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"BlogPosting","@id":"https://www.melius.com/blog/ai-video-generators-filmmakers-compared#article","headline":"AI video generators for filmmakers, compared","description":"Eight tools compared on what a director actually needs: shot control, consistency across cuts, an editorial surface, and whether the footage clears.","url":"https://www.melius.com/blog/ai-video-generators-filmmakers-compared","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.melius.com/blog/ai-video-generators-filmmakers-compared"},"datePublished":"2026-09-14","dateModified":"2026-09-14","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Melius Team","url":"https://www.melius.com"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://www.melius.com/#organization"},"isPartOf":{"@id":"https://www.melius.com/#website"},"inLanguage":"en-US","image":"https://www.melius.com/images/blog/ai-video-generators-filmmakers-compared/cover.webp","timeRequired":"PT22M","wordCount":3908,"articleSection":"Comparisons","keywords":"Comparisons"},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Blog","item":"https://www.melius.com/blog"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"AI video generators for filmmakers, compared","item":"https://www.melius.com/blog/ai-video-generators-filmmakers-compared"}]},{"@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the best AI video generator for filmmakers?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There isn't one, and the honest answer depends on which constraint binds. Runway has the best edit surface, Luma the best color and finishing, Kling the best per-shot camera control, Google Flow the best dialogue and coverage, and Moonvalley Marey and Adobe Firefly both emphasize licensed training data for commercial work. Melius combines access to leading models with image, video and audio uploads and an in-app multi-track editor, so generation and assembly can happen in one workflow."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you use AI-generated video in a film festival entry?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends on the festival, and the rules are mostly about disclosure rather than prohibition. The Academy's 99th Oscars rules require acting roles to be credited in the legal billing and demonstrably performed by humans with their consent, and screenplays to be human-authored, which closes those two categories to AI while leaving the rest of the ballot open. The same rules say generative AI tools neither help nor harm the chances of a nomination, with each branch judging the degree to which a human was at the heart of the creative authorship. Sundance, SXSW and Tribeca lean on disclosure. Cannes publishes no AI provision in its 2026 feature-film regulations at all, so check the specific festival's current rules before you submit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Sora still available?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. OpenAI discontinued the Sora web and app experiences on 26 April 2026, and its own help centre states the Sora API will be discontinued on 24 September 2026. OpenAI has not announced a successor video model. Several roundups and a few platforms still list Sora 2 as available, so treat any current recommendation of it as out of date."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long can AI-generated video clips be?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Native single-shot length runs from a few seconds to 30 depending on the model, and that number matters more than any advertised maximum. Veo 3.1 does 8 seconds, Gemini Omni Flash 10, Kling 3.0 up to 15, Luma's Ray3.2 offers 5 or 10 seconds for text-to-video and image-to-video, or up to 20 seconds when modifying existing video, and Seedance 2.5 up to 30 in a single generation. Anything longer is stitched, and stitching is where faces, wardrobe and lighting start to drift."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you own the rights to AI-generated footage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually yes on a paid plan, but the terms differ enough that they need checking per tool before anything is delivered. On Melius, you own what you create and can use it commercially, subject to the underlying model providers' terms, and Melius does not use your work to train AI models. Elsewhere the variation is real: Runway trains on your inputs and outputs on every plan below Enterprise, Luma requires an active paid subscription at the moment of generation for commercial rights to vest, and free tiers across the category generally grant the provider far broader rights than paid ones."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which AI video model is the highest quality right now?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On blind preference testing, Alibaba's Wan 3.0 and Google's Gemini Omni Flash are tied at the top, followed by MiniMax H3 Max and then the Seedance 2.0 entry. The uncomfortable part for a filmmaker is that the top of that board is almost entirely models with restricted, unclear or litigated Western commercial terms, and that neither Runway's Gen-4.5 nor any Luma model appears anywhere on the with-audio board at all, despite being the two most production-ready tools here. Quality rankings and usability rankings are not the same list."}}]}]}7:X
